# Page state

A page someone has tuned — a stock chosen, margins dragged, a duration settled on — should be
found as they left it. Navigating to the other indicator and back is not a request to start
over.

## Keep the page, do not rebuild it

Set `NavigationCacheMode="Required"` on the page root. The frame then keeps the instance and
everything in it: the fetched series, the slider positions, the scrub position.

Re-fetching instead restores *less* — a fetch brings back data, not the margins you spent two
minutes on — and pays a network round trip to arrive somewhere worse. Re-fetch only when asked.

## Caching moves where you subscribe

The constructor runs **once**; `Loaded` and `Unloaded` fire on **every** navigation. A
subscription made in the constructor and cancelled in `Unloaded` is gone from the second visit
onwards, and the page goes quietly deaf — it looks fine and stops reacting.

Subscribe in `Loaded`, unsubscribe in `Unloaded`, and remove before adding so a re-entry cannot
double up.

`VideoSettings.Changed` is wired in the constructor and never detached, which is correct for
exactly one reason: the panel is a child of the page, so it dies with it. A subscription to
anything that outlives the page does not get that.

## A flag protects only what comes after it

`VideoSettingsPanel._loading` is raised **before** the first item is added to a combo box, not
after the defaults are set. Adding an item selects it, so setting defaults first and raising
the flag second lets the first `SelectionChanged` through — and on a page that persists these,
that handler saves the default over the value being restored two lines later.

Restoring from storage is one region, and it starts at the first assignment, not at the first
one that looks like a restore.

## Handlers fire during construction

`RangeCombo.SelectedIndex = 1` in a page constructor raises `SelectionChanged` before the rest
of the page's controls have been reached by the loader. That is why `OnRangeChanged` checks
`CustomRange is null` and `OnModeChanged` checks three fields. Those null checks are not
defensive decoration; removing one is a null reference on first navigation.

## Playback and the scrub bar must not fight

`Playback` drives the position, and the scrub slider shows it. Assigning `Scrub.Value` from
playback would re-enter `OnScrub`, which stops playback — so the handler is detached for the
assignment and reattached after.

The distinction the code is preserving: a person moving the thumb means "stop and show me this
moment"; playback moving it means nothing of the kind. If a third thing ever moves it, it needs
the same treatment, and the symptom of getting it wrong is playback that stops itself after one
frame.

## Persist the question, never the answer

Chosen range, chosen stock, watchlist, video and layout parameters — persist these; re-entering
them is the friction that makes a feature go unused.

A fetched series is not persisted. It belongs to one instrument at one moment, so storing it
means deciding how long it stays true, and a stale chart is worse than an empty one.

## Run work on entry only if it is bounded

Opening a page may restore what was chosen, but it must not fetch or encode. Nobody agreed to a
network round trip, still less to writing a video, by clicking a navigation item. Restore the
selection and leave it for the button.
